# Day 10 - CI/CD Basics with GitHub Actions

## Key Concepts

CI (Continuous Integration)
→ Automatically builds and tests code whenever changes are pushed

CD (Continuous Deployment/Delivery)
→ Automatically deploys code after successful build

Pipeline
→ A sequence of automated steps (build → test → deploy)

## What is GitHub Actions

GitHub Actions is a CI/CD tool provided by GitHub to automate workflows.

* Runs workflows on events like push or pull request
* Uses YAML files to define automation steps
* Helps in building, testing, and deploying applications

## Workflow File Structure

.github/workflows/main.yml

## Basic Workflow Example

name: CI Pipeline

on:
push:
branches: [ main ]

jobs:
build:
runs-on: ubuntu-latest

```
steps:  
- name: Checkout code  
  uses: actions/checkout@v3  

- name: Run a simple command  
  run: echo "Hello from GitHub Actions"  
```

## Explanation

on: push
→ Triggers workflow when code is pushed

jobs
→ Defines tasks to be executed

runs-on
→ Specifies the environment (Ubuntu runner)

steps
→ Sequence of commands executed in order

## Workflow Execution Flow

Code Push
↓
GitHub Actions Trigger
↓
Workflow Runs
↓
Steps Execute
↓
Logs Generated

## Observations

* Every push can trigger automation
* No manual intervention needed
* Useful for testing and deployment
* Helps maintain code quality

## Debugging Learned

* Check "Actions" tab for workflow status
* YAML indentation must be correct
* Ensure correct branch is specified
* Errors can be seen in logs

## What I Understood

